00:03:13.434 --> 00:03:15.100
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I
am very excited today

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:18.910
to welcome Brian Granatir,
software developer

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:21.850
and technical lead
at Smart Parking

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:25.910
and also owner of six
dogs, living in New Zealand

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:27.229
but from Oregon.

00:03:27.229 --> 00:03:28.270
Hello, how are you doing?

00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:29.370
[LAUGHS]

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:30.230
BRIAN GRANATIR: I'm
doing wonderful.

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:31.130
Thanks for having me.

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:32.879
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Thank
you for joining us.

00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:36.640
So before we get started
talking about Smart Parking

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:40.310
and how it is to be a developer
down there, not in Australia

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:42.520
this time, but in New Zealand.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:45.410
Why don't you tell us little
bit about yourself, what you do,

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:45.910
who you are.

00:03:46.495 --> 00:03:48.800
BRIAN GRANATIR: Yeah, so,
like you said in the intro,

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:49.420
I'm Brian.

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:52.060
I've been a software developer
for, ooh, a long time,

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:54.820
probably since the beginning
of this whole cloud thing.

00:03:54.820 --> 00:04:00.184
So my original beginnings
were with Hewlett Packard,

00:04:00.184 --> 00:04:02.350
did some work there, and
then transitioned over here

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:02.980
to New Zealand.

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:04.980
And I've worked with a
couple of companies here.

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:09.610
And like you said, proud owner
of a large massive pack of dogs

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:10.750
and proud to be a kiwi.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:13.870
So that's kind of the
short line about me.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:15.010
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:15.970
All right, well, why
don't you tell us

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:17.589
a little about Smart
Parking, or I should

00:04:17.589 --> 00:04:18.055
say (AMERICAN ACCENT) parking.

00:04:18.055 --> 00:04:19.236
Oh, god.

00:04:19.236 --> 00:04:21.011
BRIAN GRANATIR: [LAUGHS]
"Smaht Pahking."

00:04:21.011 --> 00:04:22.844
MARK MANDEL: "Smaht
Pahking," I'll make sure

00:04:22.844 --> 00:04:23.600
the accents coming out.

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:25.280
Why don't you tell us a
little bit about Smart Parking

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:27.350
and what does it do,
what's it all about.

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:29.683
BRIAN GRANATIR: Yeah, so Smart
Parking is a company that

00:04:29.683 --> 00:04:31.210
develops parking technologies.

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:32.860
So for most of our
clients, that's

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:37.540
in-ground sensors, or what
we call camera gateways.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:40.660
And we've been developing that
for, oof, years and years now.

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:45.190
And we have installations in
New Zealand, Australia, the UK,

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:49.957
Dubai, Singapore, and
a few other places.

00:04:49.957 --> 00:04:51.540
I can never keep
track of all of them.

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:52.840
And so we've been
working on these

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:55.381
for years and the services and
the mobile apps and everything

00:04:55.381 --> 00:04:56.090
to support that.

00:04:56.090 --> 00:04:56.950
And then at the
start of this year,

00:04:56.950 --> 00:04:58.810
we've begun our
new endeavor, which

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:01.600
was to create a Smart
Cities Platform.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:05.020
And the goal of that is to take
all of these different devices

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:07.030
and messages that you
might get from a city,

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:12.580
be it traffic, parking,
weather, energy grids, lighting,

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:14.680
and kind of bring them
all together, kind

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:17.590
of connecting IoT of things
together to one platform

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:19.380
and then deriving
meaning off of that.

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:20.130
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:23.080
I'd love to dig more into what
actually this Smart Parking

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:23.590
stuff is.

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:25.420
You said you're doing analytics?

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:27.820
Is it just like who
parks where, what, why?

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:29.650
Or is it like real-time
stuff, that when

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:31.360
people come to a
car park, they can

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:32.567
be directed to free parking?

00:05:32.567 --> 00:05:34.150
Like this actually
sounds really cool,

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:35.835
so I'm super curious
about what it does.

00:05:35.835 --> 00:05:37.210
BRIAN GRANATIR:
Yeah, definitely.

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:39.350
Yes, so what we do is we
offer network services.

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:41.286
So the one that's very
common is sometimes

00:05:41.286 --> 00:05:42.660
you'll go to a
parking structure,

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:44.590
and they'll tell you
there's 12 bays available

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:47.110
on the second floor, but
there's 150 bays available

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:48.370
on the third floor.

00:05:48.370 --> 00:05:50.380
And our sensors are
detecting those vehicles

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:51.920
arriving and going.

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:54.430
They also help with
enforcement, so very common

00:05:54.430 --> 00:05:57.469
in downtown areas or near
government buildings.

00:05:57.469 --> 00:06:00.010
They'll put in our sensors and
say, well, you got 30 minutes.

00:06:00.010 --> 00:06:01.719
And then after you
go on for 30 minutes,

00:06:01.719 --> 00:06:03.760
we can let an enforcement
officer know to go over

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:05.934
and ticket the person.

00:06:05.934 --> 00:06:07.600
So sometimes when you
get those tickets,

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:09.760
you can curse our names.

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:11.890
But then also we offer a
variety of other services.

00:06:11.890 --> 00:06:13.639
Like I mentioned, we
do have a mobile app

00:06:13.639 --> 00:06:15.430
that people can use
and say, hey, while I'm

00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:19.097
driving downtown, I want to find
parking near to the stadium.

00:06:19.097 --> 00:06:19.930
Can you help me out?

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:22.055
And we'll tell you what
the parking availability is

00:06:22.055 --> 00:06:25.470
in various areas and help to
navigate the driver to it.

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:26.390
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:06:26.390 --> 00:06:28.780
And I'm curious.

00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:32.000
All of this, what kind of
technology are you using?

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:33.450
Are you using the cloud?

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:35.650
It sounds like
internet of things.

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:37.047
Tell us a little bit about that.

00:06:37.047 --> 00:06:37.880
BRIAN GRANATIR: Yes.

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:40.830
So the old platform, so
what we professionally

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:44.590
refer to as a version 2,
was all 100% home-built.

00:06:44.590 --> 00:06:48.010
So it's using
custom-built devices

00:06:48.010 --> 00:06:50.740
that are installed on the
ground and custom gateways that

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:54.600
broadcast the stuff back
to our network services

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:55.660
and goes to our servers.

00:06:55.660 --> 00:06:58.420
We did do a transition of
those servers out to AWS.

00:06:58.420 --> 00:07:00.320
And that was kind of
the structure that

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:01.570
worked for us for a long time,

00:07:01.570 --> 00:07:03.820
But now with the
new platform, we've

00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:06.400
actually built the whole thing
out on Google Cloud Platform,

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:09.667
which gave us a whole
new set of opportunities.

00:07:09.667 --> 00:07:12.250
And this is when we started to
embrace the internet of things,

00:07:12.250 --> 00:07:12.750
right?

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:15.062
Not just our device,
but any device

00:07:15.062 --> 00:07:17.020
that wants to be able to
kind of join our party

00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:18.940
is able to send us the
messages, and we're

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:22.150
able to aggregate,
analyze, and act upon all

00:07:22.150 --> 00:07:23.164
those different devices.

00:07:23.164 --> 00:07:24.080
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:26.160
So how do you do that?

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:27.042
[LAUGHS]

00:07:27.042 --> 00:07:29.500
BRIAN GRANATIR: [LAUGHS] Well,
that's the real magic of it.

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:32.110
So the real trick to it
is first and foremost, you

00:07:32.110 --> 00:07:33.410
got to get the data in, right?

00:07:33.410 --> 00:07:35.440
So you have to get
it all coming to us.

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:37.140
And that's where the
IoT thing comes in.

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:38.723
And there's a lot
of different devices

00:07:38.723 --> 00:07:40.592
out there, everything
from our sensors,

00:07:40.592 --> 00:07:42.300
which are parking
sensors in the grounds,

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:44.590
to there's even trash
cans that will tell you

00:07:44.590 --> 00:07:45.760
when they're too full.

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:49.854
There's lighting sensors,
the IoT, the actual devices.

00:07:49.854 --> 00:07:52.270
So we need to get them coming
into us, first and foremost.

00:07:52.270 --> 00:07:54.610
And that's what we
use IoT Core for.

00:07:54.610 --> 00:07:56.211
So Google, I think, just made--

00:07:56.211 --> 00:07:56.710
they did.

00:07:56.710 --> 00:08:00.470
They made their announcement to
make the public for the beta.

00:08:00.470 --> 00:08:02.690
We've been using it now
for a couple of months.

00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:05.020
And that was what really
helped us get the data in.

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:06.640
Then once you get
the data in, that's

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:07.750
where you start having
to say, all right,

00:08:07.750 --> 00:08:09.350
how am I going to
normalize this?

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:12.280
How am I going to take data
from trash cans and data

00:08:12.280 --> 00:08:14.590
from parking and the
data from traffic

00:08:14.590 --> 00:08:15.830
to be able to make a picture?

00:08:15.830 --> 00:08:17.680
So one of the things
that we like to do

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:21.740
is we like to use examples of
what a city might do for this.

00:08:21.740 --> 00:08:23.920
So we might be able to
detect, all right, well,

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:28.860
suddenly, at 9:00 PM we see
a bunch of people departing.

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:31.090
All the parking's emptying
out in the downtown area.

00:08:31.090 --> 00:08:32.850
Maybe a concert's emptied.

00:08:32.850 --> 00:08:35.260
In our platform, the idea
that we can detect that

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:37.343
and say, hey, well, we see
a whole bunch of people

00:08:37.343 --> 00:08:39.100
are leaving the downtown area.

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:42.370
Let's change our traffic
lights to help outbound traffic

00:08:42.370 --> 00:08:43.347
leaving downtown.

00:08:43.347 --> 00:08:45.430
So that's the kind of idea
of a smart city, right?

00:08:45.430 --> 00:08:47.992
Taking data from one thing,
putting it somewhere else,

00:08:47.992 --> 00:08:49.450
taking the fact
that we've detected

00:08:49.450 --> 00:08:53.260
there's a giant rainstorm over
on I-5, and what I want to do

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:55.540
is reduce the
speeds for everyone

00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:57.220
there on the digital signs.

00:08:57.220 --> 00:09:00.100
So you have to say, all
right, we've got the data.

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:01.447
We have the goal.

00:09:01.447 --> 00:09:03.280
Now how do we get that
all to work together?

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:05.620
And that's been the kind of
magic we've been working on.

00:09:05.620 --> 00:09:08.530
We've been using an event-driven
architecture to do that.

00:09:08.530 --> 00:09:10.600
So we look at
everything as events.

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:14.630
And we also use what's called
spatiotemporal tagging,

00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:16.400
so it's space and time.

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:18.160
Yeah, yeah, there's
some amazing articles.

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:19.618
You drop that word
in conversation,

00:09:19.618 --> 00:09:22.450
people are like, ooh.

00:09:22.450 --> 00:09:24.970
We use spatiotemporal tagging.

00:09:24.970 --> 00:09:26.710
And then we use
temporal reasoning.

00:09:26.710 --> 00:09:27.890
Those are all great things.

00:09:27.890 --> 00:09:29.830
There's some amazing
white papers.

00:09:29.830 --> 00:09:31.750
But it basically
comes into the idea

00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:34.840
that everything comes into
us, and it knows the place,

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:36.040
and it knows the time.

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:38.500
And then what we do is we use
the event-driven architecture

00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:41.700
to make these aggregates and
start clumping them together.

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:44.180
We make these basically
events of events.

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:46.814
So without getting
too technical,

00:09:46.814 --> 00:09:47.730
in temporal reasoning.

00:09:47.730 --> 00:09:49.060
MARK MANDEL: Get technical
as technical as you like.

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:49.500
[LAUGHS]

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:50.160
BRIAN GRANATIR: [LAUGHS] Yeah.

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:50.380
Yeah.

00:09:50.380 --> 00:09:51.880
Well, I highly
recommend-- there's

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:54.280
some great papers on
temporal reasoning out there.

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:56.080
It's actually becoming
a really big thing.

00:09:56.080 --> 00:09:57.740
Because with AI and
machine learning,

00:09:57.740 --> 00:09:59.830
which is another thing
that we like to leverage,

00:09:59.830 --> 00:10:02.410
you start looking at things
in the relationship of what

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:03.081
happened when.

00:10:03.081 --> 00:10:05.330
And what you do is you put
them in these things called

00:10:05.330 --> 00:10:05.930
intervals.

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:07.810
And intervals are made
up of smaller events.

00:10:07.810 --> 00:10:09.770
So for example, a lecture
could be an interval.

00:10:09.770 --> 00:10:11.311
And during that
lecture, I could say,

00:10:11.311 --> 00:10:13.400
introduction,
Brian makes a joke,

00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:15.890
Q&amp;A, and then wrap-up, right?

00:10:15.890 --> 00:10:16.930
And that's an interval.

00:10:16.930 --> 00:10:18.122
It's a sequence of events.

00:10:18.122 --> 00:10:19.580
And then what you
can do is you can

00:10:19.580 --> 00:10:21.380
use those intervals
in relationship

00:10:21.380 --> 00:10:22.790
to bigger and smaller intervals.

00:10:22.790 --> 00:10:24.623
So you can say, hey,
well, an interval could

00:10:24.623 --> 00:10:27.330
be work day, which is made up of
four lectures and a lunch time,

00:10:27.330 --> 00:10:27.950
right?

00:10:27.950 --> 00:10:29.325
And then what you
do is you start

00:10:29.325 --> 00:10:31.310
using logic that
says, all right, well,

00:10:31.310 --> 00:10:35.330
if I've seen A happen before
B, but c hasn't happened yet,

00:10:35.330 --> 00:10:37.800
then I trigger some
sort of behavior.

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:39.770
So it's easy to
say those examples.

00:10:39.770 --> 00:10:41.450
Getting it actually
implemented in a way

00:10:41.450 --> 00:10:43.130
that people can
easily-- normal humans

00:10:43.130 --> 00:10:46.900
can-- interact with it
is kind of the trick.

00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:49.050
And we've used a couple
of methodologies to that.

00:10:49.050 --> 00:10:51.790
So that's kind of the overview.

00:10:51.790 --> 00:10:53.780
Get the data in
through IoT Core.

00:10:53.780 --> 00:10:56.590
Do some big, old processing
with event architecture.

00:10:56.590 --> 00:11:00.950
Do some temporal reasoning
on spatiotemporal tags.

00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:03.770
And there you go,
smart city, that easy.

00:11:03.770 --> 00:11:07.130
MARK MANDEL: So you're saying
get the data in from IoT Core.

00:11:07.130 --> 00:11:08.120
Where does that go?

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:09.415
Does that go to like GCP?

00:11:09.415 --> 00:11:11.540
Does it go to a particular
GCP product or products?

00:11:11.540 --> 00:11:12.260
BRIAN GRANATIR: Yes.

00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:12.475
Yes.

00:11:12.475 --> 00:11:14.141
MARK MANDEL: So how
does that part work?

00:11:14.141 --> 00:11:16.046
BRIAN GRANATIR:
Yeah, and this is

00:11:16.046 --> 00:11:18.440
why I was happy to do this
podcast because a lot of this

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:21.650
is the magic of actual
GCP coming in, right?

00:11:21.650 --> 00:11:23.587
So one of the things
that we did was

00:11:23.587 --> 00:11:25.170
when we were sitting
there and saying,

00:11:25.170 --> 00:11:26.450
all right, we know
what we want to do it.

00:11:26.450 --> 00:11:28.520
We want to get this
data in from cities.

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:31.340
One of the biggest concerns
was just the sheer volume

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:32.310
of the data, right?

00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:34.520
Just parking alone,
you have hundreds

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:38.700
of events per
single lot per hour,

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:40.700
right, cars coming
and going, staying.

00:11:40.700 --> 00:11:43.490
And our sensors don't send you
messages that are just like,

00:11:43.490 --> 00:11:44.930
oh, the vehicles left.

00:11:44.930 --> 00:11:45.710
The vehicles come.

00:11:45.710 --> 00:11:47.930
No, they're actually reporting
as often as they get changes

00:11:47.930 --> 00:11:48.850
in the sensor, right?

00:11:48.850 --> 00:11:51.530
So an IR or a magnetic
change could be caused

00:11:51.530 --> 00:11:52.840
by a whole bunch of things.

00:11:52.840 --> 00:11:54.470
It's sending the message to us.

00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:57.700
So just parking alone, let alone
putting in traffic, weather,

00:11:57.700 --> 00:12:02.755
or any other data is just
a huge volume of data.

00:12:02.755 --> 00:12:04.130
And so this is
one of the reasons

00:12:04.130 --> 00:12:05.480
that we turned to Google.

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:07.520
Google's products
and services are

00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:10.280
built to work with
Google's problems, right?

00:12:10.280 --> 00:12:11.600
You're talking Google scale.

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:14.660
Cities are pretty big, not
quite Google scale, right?

00:12:14.660 --> 00:12:16.460
So if we can use
systems that will

00:12:16.460 --> 00:12:18.260
handle the entire
internet, they'll

00:12:18.260 --> 00:12:19.400
probably work on cities.

00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:21.430
There's still pretty big,
but not quite as big.

00:12:21.430 --> 00:12:22.880
So that was the first thing.

00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:25.320
We said, all right,
we want to use Google.

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:29.000
But fortunately, Google's
different services

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:31.070
have best practices
baked in, right?

00:12:31.070 --> 00:12:31.880
They really do.

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:33.380
They have this
kind of philosophy.

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:36.360
Not only did they design
it to meet their needs,

00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:39.092
they also designed it to meet
the kind of sciencey side

00:12:39.092 --> 00:12:39.800
of things, right?

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:42.110
That there's the best
practices baked in.

00:12:42.110 --> 00:12:44.840
So we said, all right,
let's use Google.

00:12:44.840 --> 00:12:46.637
That's going to meet our scale.

00:12:46.637 --> 00:12:49.220
I'd never done anything with GCP
before starting this project.

00:12:49.220 --> 00:12:54.142
I was an AWS person, did
AWS for a decade, loved it.

00:12:54.142 --> 00:12:55.850
And then we said,
well, we're going to go

00:12:55.850 --> 00:12:57.470
try Google for this thing.

00:12:57.470 --> 00:13:01.190
And then you just start putting
the boxes together, right?

00:13:01.190 --> 00:13:03.330
Like I said, it sounds
really, really complicated.

00:13:03.330 --> 00:13:05.490
But building it's actually
really, really fast.

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:08.300
So you bring in
messages from IoT Core.

00:13:08.300 --> 00:13:10.280
And then they go into a Pub/Sub.

00:13:10.280 --> 00:13:13.250
So people are maybe familiar
with the queues that Pub/Sub

00:13:13.250 --> 00:13:14.750
subs are-- love them--

00:13:14.750 --> 00:13:16.800
the very backbone of what we do.

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:18.470
And in many ways,
the very backbone

00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:20.540
of a real event-driven
architecture

00:13:20.540 --> 00:13:23.120
is being able to have
these kinds of queues

00:13:23.120 --> 00:13:24.260
that people subscribe to.

00:13:24.260 --> 00:13:26.280
And they can get
notified of events.

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:27.950
So messages come in.

00:13:27.950 --> 00:13:29.000
Cars left.

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:30.690
Traffic's going up.

00:13:30.690 --> 00:13:32.990
We've detected
increased precipitation.

00:13:32.990 --> 00:13:34.310
Those messages come in.

00:13:34.310 --> 00:13:36.620
They get routed into
queues in Pub/Sub.

00:13:36.620 --> 00:13:38.640
And then the systems
that subscribe

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:39.860
to it are Cloud Functions.

00:13:39.860 --> 00:13:44.390
So Cloud Functions are Google's
serverless architecture.

00:13:44.390 --> 00:13:47.290
Oh, I love the Cloud Functions.

00:13:47.290 --> 00:13:47.990
They're amazing.

00:13:47.990 --> 00:13:51.140
Like, if you haven't written
anything in Cloud Functions,

00:13:51.140 --> 00:13:53.520
I recommend anyone who
does web services do it.

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:56.340
It's similar to AWS's Lambda.

00:13:56.340 --> 00:14:00.830
But, again, it's built
in to kind of deliver

00:14:00.830 --> 00:14:02.580
this through-put,
this massive scale.

00:14:02.580 --> 00:14:04.209
So messages come in.

00:14:04.209 --> 00:14:06.500
They get routed out through
the queues through Pub/Sub.

00:14:06.500 --> 00:14:08.630
And then we have Cloud
Functions pick them up.

00:14:08.630 --> 00:14:10.130
And then they start doing
their processing, right?

00:14:10.130 --> 00:14:11.810
They do one thing, and they
do one thing really well.

00:14:11.810 --> 00:14:13.430
So this is the dream, right?

00:14:13.430 --> 00:14:17.180
You may have heard we
want microservices, right?

00:14:17.180 --> 00:14:18.650
Everyone's like that's the goal.

00:14:18.650 --> 00:14:19.906
We want microservices.

00:14:19.906 --> 00:14:21.530
And I've tried building
micro services.

00:14:21.530 --> 00:14:23.230
Now this is my third attempt.

00:14:23.230 --> 00:14:24.813
And it is the first
time I've actually

00:14:24.813 --> 00:14:26.930
been able to achieve
true microservices.

00:14:26.930 --> 00:14:28.770
And that's because
a couple of things.

00:14:28.770 --> 00:14:30.620
One, you run these
Cloud Functions

00:14:30.620 --> 00:14:34.220
through little serverless
functions, right?

00:14:34.220 --> 00:14:35.430
They do one thing.

00:14:35.430 --> 00:14:36.849
They do that thing really well.

00:14:36.849 --> 00:14:38.390
They come online
when they're needed.

00:14:38.390 --> 00:14:39.960
And then they shut down.

00:14:39.960 --> 00:14:41.510
And so that's the step, right?

00:14:41.510 --> 00:14:45.481
And so our system, which was all
that complicated stuff I said,

00:14:45.481 --> 00:14:45.980
right?

00:14:45.980 --> 00:14:48.800
All the fancy words I
used is nothing more

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:51.402
than a set of dots
connected together that are

00:14:51.402 --> 00:14:52.610
these little Cloud Functions.

00:14:52.610 --> 00:14:54.943
And every single one of them,
with the exception of one,

00:14:54.943 --> 00:14:56.840
which is the one
function I hate,

00:14:56.840 --> 00:14:58.320
is under 100 lines of code.

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:01.550
In fact, almost all of them are
under 30 lines of code, right?

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:04.360
They literally bring the
thing in, do one thing,

00:15:04.360 --> 00:15:06.580
and then pass it on, right?

00:15:06.580 --> 00:15:09.040
And so that's the
trick, right, taking

00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:11.286
a very complicated thing,
and then breaking it down.

00:15:11.286 --> 00:15:12.910
So everything that
comes in our system,

00:15:12.910 --> 00:15:14.770
for the adventure in
architecture junkies,

00:15:14.770 --> 00:15:17.590
commands coming in, we
convert them to events.

00:15:17.590 --> 00:15:19.867
And then we just have things
react to those events,

00:15:19.867 --> 00:15:21.950
right, creating new commands,
creating new events,

00:15:21.950 --> 00:15:23.810
creating new commands,
creating new events.

00:15:23.810 --> 00:15:25.351
And the secret sauce
comes into being

00:15:25.351 --> 00:15:26.950
able to find a way
to say, I'm going

00:15:26.950 --> 00:15:32.530
to use that geospatial, that
spatiotemporal data so the time

00:15:32.530 --> 00:15:35.770
and the location to be able
to start grouping together

00:15:35.770 --> 00:15:39.940
these events and then try to
grab meaning from the larger

00:15:39.940 --> 00:15:41.650
view of what a city's
going on, what's

00:15:41.650 --> 00:15:44.371
the actual living, happening
events within the city.

00:15:44.394 --> 00:15:45.310
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:15:45.310 --> 00:15:47.890
So that give us is a
actually pretty specific idea

00:15:47.890 --> 00:15:51.140
even of how you do what you do.

00:15:51.140 --> 00:15:53.380
But there is a
piece of all of this

00:15:53.380 --> 00:15:55.360
that I'm actually
very interested in.

00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:59.390
You were saying, like, once we
have the data, then we do this.

00:15:59.390 --> 00:16:00.580
How do you get that data?

00:16:00.580 --> 00:16:03.482
How does the data goes from
the sensors to that Pub/Sub?

00:16:03.482 --> 00:16:05.440
Could you tell us a little
bit more about that?

00:16:05.440 --> 00:16:05.650
BRIAN GRANATIR: Yeah.

00:16:05.650 --> 00:16:07.757
And that's where
IoT Core comes in.

00:16:07.757 --> 00:16:09.340
So this is the thing
that was the kind

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:12.650
of the secret, the thing that
kind of unlocked it for us.

00:16:12.650 --> 00:16:15.069
So previously what
we did is we said,

00:16:15.069 --> 00:16:16.485
well, we're going
to get a device.

00:16:16.485 --> 00:16:18.220
And then it's going to
send us information.

00:16:18.220 --> 00:16:19.660
It's going to go into
one of our gateways,

00:16:19.660 --> 00:16:21.130
and we're going to handle it.

00:16:21.130 --> 00:16:24.507
But now with IoT Core, able to
say, well, what we want to do

00:16:24.507 --> 00:16:26.590
is we want to let any
device send us a message in.

00:16:26.590 --> 00:16:30.280
So IoT core is basically
a management platform

00:16:30.280 --> 00:16:31.270
for devices.

00:16:31.270 --> 00:16:32.320
Google has one.

00:16:32.320 --> 00:16:33.290
AWS has one.

00:16:33.290 --> 00:16:38.030
I think it's called AWS IoT
Platform, for reference.

00:16:38.030 --> 00:16:40.340
And it fills in this
new gap, where you say,

00:16:40.340 --> 00:16:43.780
well, I've got a device, and I
need it to get me this message.

00:16:43.780 --> 00:16:46.060
Now, that used to
be really painful.

00:16:46.060 --> 00:16:48.550
But IoT Core gives
you the security,

00:16:48.550 --> 00:16:50.469
which is if I can
say, hey, something's

00:16:50.469 --> 00:16:52.510
giving me the security,
and it's Google security,

00:16:52.510 --> 00:16:55.330
so you know it's really good.

00:16:55.330 --> 00:16:56.586
So they give you the security.

00:16:56.586 --> 00:16:59.210
They also let you do the device
management, where you say, hey,

00:16:59.210 --> 00:17:00.490
I'm going to install
on your device.

00:17:00.490 --> 00:17:01.610
Here are the credentials.

00:17:01.610 --> 00:17:02.890
It makes the communication.

00:17:02.890 --> 00:17:04.720
It sets up all the routing.

00:17:04.720 --> 00:17:07.044
And then you can then
start sending messages in,

00:17:07.044 --> 00:17:09.460
and you also have a feedback
loop so you can send messages

00:17:09.460 --> 00:17:09.970
back out.

00:17:09.970 --> 00:17:12.130
So let's say I need to
push a firmware update

00:17:12.130 --> 00:17:13.770
or I need to do
something like that,

00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:15.624
I handle that two-way
communication.

00:17:15.624 --> 00:17:17.540
So I don't know how
familiar you are with IoT,

00:17:17.540 --> 00:17:20.950
but IoT basically consists
of sensors, devices,

00:17:20.950 --> 00:17:22.329
connected to gateways.

00:17:22.329 --> 00:17:24.364
They may be co-located
with the sensor.

00:17:24.364 --> 00:17:26.530
But that's the thing that's
responsible for handling

00:17:26.530 --> 00:17:27.640
the communication.

00:17:27.640 --> 00:17:30.832
At this point, it just
talks to IoT core.

00:17:30.832 --> 00:17:32.290
There's actually
a lot of companies

00:17:32.290 --> 00:17:34.270
that are making gateways.

00:17:34.270 --> 00:17:36.220
Intel's been one of
them and a couple others

00:17:36.220 --> 00:17:36.880
that you guys have--

00:17:36.880 --> 00:17:37.990
I'm pretty sure you've
got a good partner

00:17:37.990 --> 00:17:40.750
list on the website that make
the gateways that fill in that.

00:17:40.750 --> 00:17:43.202
So you can say, well, I've
got a device over here,

00:17:43.202 --> 00:17:45.160
but I didn't really think
about how I was going

00:17:45.160 --> 00:17:46.810
to handle the communication.

00:17:46.810 --> 00:17:47.920
They do gateways.

00:17:47.920 --> 00:17:51.640
Our system, we offer a smart
spot for our smart cloud, which

00:17:51.640 --> 00:17:53.690
is a generic gateway
that allows you to say,

00:17:53.690 --> 00:17:55.942
hey, I'm going to accept
broadcasts from any device,

00:17:55.942 --> 00:17:57.400
package them up,
and then send them

00:17:57.400 --> 00:18:00.289
into the smart
cloud, or IoT Core,

00:18:00.289 --> 00:18:02.080
management's front end
or the back end that

00:18:02.080 --> 00:18:03.620
goes off IoT Core.

00:18:03.620 --> 00:18:05.980
And then so you basically,
you kind of get this thing.

00:18:05.980 --> 00:18:06.650
You get devices.

00:18:06.650 --> 00:18:08.080
They're sending you this data.

00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:11.114
And I'm sure like a lot
of people, when you first

00:18:11.114 --> 00:18:12.030
heard about IoT Core--

00:18:12.030 --> 00:18:13.200
well, maybe, it's just me.

00:18:13.200 --> 00:18:14.980
I'm kind of skeptical about
a lot of stuff, right?

00:18:14.980 --> 00:18:17.230
When people told me that
cloud was the next big thing,

00:18:17.230 --> 00:18:19.380
I'm like, mm, it's
just servers, guys.

00:18:19.380 --> 00:18:21.796
It's just-- but then
now I love the cloud.

00:18:21.796 --> 00:18:22.920
I couldn't live without it.

00:18:22.920 --> 00:18:25.090
Then when they said,
oh, here comes IoT,

00:18:25.090 --> 00:18:27.250
and I'm like, well, IoT,
what, smart fridges are

00:18:27.250 --> 00:18:29.520
going to connect with
my smart shopping cart.

00:18:29.520 --> 00:18:31.270
It's going to connect
everything together,

00:18:31.270 --> 00:18:33.370
and everything's going
to work magically.

00:18:33.370 --> 00:18:34.330
Well, it's one of the
things where you're like,

00:18:34.330 --> 00:18:35.080
it's a pipe dream.

00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:36.880
Give it another 20 years, right?

00:18:36.880 --> 00:18:38.650
But the introduction
of these kind

00:18:38.650 --> 00:18:42.850
of platforms, these management
services, this IoT Core

00:18:42.850 --> 00:18:44.620
has basically
removed that hurdle.

00:18:44.620 --> 00:18:47.290
Now you can say, hey,
I've got it installed.

00:18:47.290 --> 00:18:49.090
It's connected to these devices.

00:18:49.090 --> 00:18:51.820
They're getting the security
certificates installed

00:18:51.820 --> 00:18:53.290
through the process
of just getting

00:18:53.290 --> 00:18:54.792
them the installation process.

00:18:54.792 --> 00:18:56.500
And then the data just
starts flowing in.

00:18:56.500 --> 00:18:58.980
And IoT Core allows you to
direct that into a Pub/Sub,

00:18:58.980 --> 00:18:59.480
right?

00:18:59.480 --> 00:19:01.188
It's connected directly
into the service.

00:19:01.188 --> 00:19:02.810
So IoT Core allows you do that.

00:19:02.810 --> 00:19:06.820
So to give a kind of
assessment of how it went,

00:19:06.820 --> 00:19:09.580
we started with our
own homegrown system.

00:19:09.580 --> 00:19:12.610
We got IoT Core into
the private beta.

00:19:12.610 --> 00:19:14.710
It took one of our
engineers, Ken,

00:19:14.710 --> 00:19:17.170
I think it took them three days
to transfer everything over

00:19:17.170 --> 00:19:19.870
from the old system to IoT Core.

00:19:19.870 --> 00:19:22.720
So we went from using
our stuff to using

00:19:22.720 --> 00:19:25.240
IoT Core, which has
Google security,

00:19:25.240 --> 00:19:27.970
hooks directly into the
Pub/Subs, everything,

00:19:27.970 --> 00:19:29.069
three days, one engineer.

00:19:29.069 --> 00:19:31.360
So that was one of the things
that just kind of sold me

00:19:31.360 --> 00:19:32.200
on the whole thing.

00:19:32.200 --> 00:19:32.620
And guess what?

00:19:32.620 --> 00:19:33.970
We've had the thing
up and running.

00:19:33.970 --> 00:19:34.960
I haven't touched since.

00:19:34.960 --> 00:19:36.010
I haven't done a thing to it.

00:19:36.010 --> 00:19:37.218
We just install more devices.

00:19:37.218 --> 00:19:38.430
We install more gateways.

00:19:38.430 --> 00:19:39.370
That's it.

00:19:39.370 --> 00:19:40.750
And we haven't had any problems.

00:19:40.750 --> 00:19:42.166
We haven't had
anything fall over.

00:19:42.166 --> 00:19:43.400
We haven't had any issues.

00:19:43.400 --> 00:19:44.108
It's just worked.

00:19:44.108 --> 00:19:47.094
It's just sending me a ton
of data, and it's that easy.

00:19:47.094 --> 00:19:48.760
MARK MANDEL: So I'm
kind of curious now,

00:19:48.760 --> 00:19:52.330
as well, because I know nothing
about the IoT side of things.

00:19:52.330 --> 00:19:55.240
I'm guessing you have some
kind of manufacturing process

00:19:55.240 --> 00:19:59.230
for building these devices or
constructing them in some way.

00:19:59.230 --> 00:20:01.150
Is there some special
hooks into IoT?

00:20:01.150 --> 00:20:04.652
Like, how do you get the IoT
Core thing on the device?

00:20:04.652 --> 00:20:06.110
You must have some
kind of factory.

00:20:06.110 --> 00:20:06.740
Like, how does that work?

00:20:06.740 --> 00:20:07.040
BRIAN GRANATIR: Yeah.

00:20:07.040 --> 00:20:07.610
Yeah.

00:20:07.610 --> 00:20:11.660
So we do have custom-built
sensors for ours--

00:20:11.660 --> 00:20:13.310
so the ones that we do install.

00:20:13.310 --> 00:20:15.351
But that actually
isn't required.

00:20:15.351 --> 00:20:17.600
You don't have to actually
go this whole custom route.

00:20:17.600 --> 00:20:20.900
So what you can do is you
basically have two parts.

00:20:20.900 --> 00:20:23.750
So part one is the thing
that's doing the sensing.

00:20:23.750 --> 00:20:27.860
So it could be something that
detects the amount of particles

00:20:27.860 --> 00:20:30.514
of bacteria in your fridge
to tell you how much you're

00:20:30.514 --> 00:20:31.180
butter's rotten.

00:20:31.180 --> 00:20:32.620
I don't know.

00:20:32.620 --> 00:20:34.970
Or it could be a sensor that
tells you how many cars are

00:20:34.970 --> 00:20:36.230
driving over a road.

00:20:36.230 --> 00:20:38.810
Or it could be something
that tells you parking.

00:20:38.810 --> 00:20:39.800
You build the sensor.

00:20:39.800 --> 00:20:40.700
You build the device.

00:20:40.700 --> 00:20:42.158
And then all you
need to do is have

00:20:42.158 --> 00:20:44.450
something that takes
that information,

00:20:44.450 --> 00:20:46.800
packages it up, and
then sends it through.

00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:48.960
So those are the gateways.

00:20:48.960 --> 00:20:51.639
So you can be a kind of
separation of the two.

00:20:51.639 --> 00:20:53.180
You don't have to
build both of them.

00:20:53.180 --> 00:20:54.388
They don't need to be custom.

00:20:54.388 --> 00:20:55.388
The sensors usually are.

00:20:55.388 --> 00:20:57.262
But the gateways definitely
don't need to be.

00:20:57.262 --> 00:20:58.980
But you can make it
be anything you want.

00:20:58.980 --> 00:21:02.180
So one of my
developers right now,

00:21:02.180 --> 00:21:04.100
we're prototyping
some other stuff.

00:21:04.100 --> 00:21:07.110
And he just made it so that
his webcam is an IoT thing.

00:21:07.110 --> 00:21:10.400
And any time a blue object
passes in front of it,

00:21:10.400 --> 00:21:12.800
it detects that a blue object
has passed in front of it.

00:21:12.800 --> 00:21:16.190
We're using Google's Vision API
to detect the analysis, right?

00:21:16.190 --> 00:21:17.871
So somebody blue
passes in front of it.

00:21:17.871 --> 00:21:20.370
One of our developers happens
to like to wear a blue jacket.

00:21:20.370 --> 00:21:21.890
So he walks in front of it.

00:21:21.890 --> 00:21:24.320
Whenever that camera
detects it, it packages up,

00:21:24.320 --> 00:21:27.710
sends it through a
gateway, which it's just

00:21:27.710 --> 00:21:28.970
self-contained in the code.

00:21:28.970 --> 00:21:30.350
And it literally hits IoT Core.

00:21:30.350 --> 00:21:33.620
So his webcam, with a little
bit of code behind it,

00:21:33.620 --> 00:21:35.420
is literally an IoT
Core device, right,

00:21:35.420 --> 00:21:38.790
designed to detect the
presence of blue objects.

00:21:38.790 --> 00:21:41.300
So you could do anything you
want for these IoT things.

00:21:41.300 --> 00:21:43.460
And you don't want to
be restrictive, right?

00:21:43.460 --> 00:21:45.080
If you say, oh, you've got
to have a special gateway,

00:21:45.080 --> 00:21:47.621
or you've got to have a special
handshake or anything, that's

00:21:47.621 --> 00:21:50.540
where you start basically
making it a walled garden.

00:21:50.540 --> 00:21:51.770
But you don't want that.

00:21:51.770 --> 00:21:54.519
So like our smart spots
are designed to facilitate.

00:21:54.519 --> 00:21:56.060
What you usually
see in cities is you

00:21:56.060 --> 00:21:57.920
see radio frequencies, right?

00:21:57.920 --> 00:21:59.690
So you don't get to run wires.

00:21:59.690 --> 00:22:02.930
And very often the Wi-Fi
stuff is not going to give you

00:22:02.930 --> 00:22:03.859
the kind of--

00:22:03.859 --> 00:22:05.900
well, it just hasn't been
deployed across cities.

00:22:05.900 --> 00:22:07.550
But like our in-ground sensors?

00:22:07.550 --> 00:22:08.740
Radio frequencies.

00:22:08.740 --> 00:22:10.310
These smart trash cans?

00:22:10.310 --> 00:22:11.570
Radio frequencies.

00:22:11.570 --> 00:22:13.460
So our smart spot's
designed to sit there

00:22:13.460 --> 00:22:15.420
and say, well, I'm going
to take in any message

00:22:15.420 --> 00:22:16.670
coming over radio frequencies.

00:22:16.670 --> 00:22:17.937
I'm going to package it up.

00:22:17.937 --> 00:22:19.520
I'm going to wrap
it with information.

00:22:19.520 --> 00:22:21.919
And then I'm going to send
it to the smart cloud.

00:22:21.919 --> 00:22:24.210
And then we take it, and then
when it gets to back end,

00:22:24.210 --> 00:22:26.168
we're able to say, oh,
that's from a trash can.

00:22:26.168 --> 00:22:26.890
Let's decode it.

00:22:26.890 --> 00:22:28.640
And then let's turn
it into a command that

00:22:28.640 --> 00:22:31.070
goes into an event, and
blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

00:22:31.070 --> 00:22:32.278
MARK MANDEL: All right, cool.

00:22:32.278 --> 00:22:34.666
So getting in all this data,
where are you storing it?

00:22:34.666 --> 00:22:37.040
Obviously, you're doing a lot
of analytics and processing

00:22:37.040 --> 00:22:38.450
and sorts of other fun
stuff over the top.

00:22:38.450 --> 00:22:40.010
Like where does this
actually end up?

00:22:40.010 --> 00:22:41.750
BRIAN GRANATIR: Yeah, and
that's the other thing.

00:22:41.750 --> 00:22:43.958
So I think one of the things
you have to also look at

00:22:43.958 --> 00:22:47.150
is just the scale, like I
said, right, lots of data.

00:22:47.150 --> 00:22:50.100
So for us, what we
wanted to do was say,

00:22:50.100 --> 00:22:53.580
well, we want to be able to
make the databases two things.

00:22:53.580 --> 00:22:56.540
One, we need our databases
to be very simple,

00:22:56.540 --> 00:22:58.800
because I don't want to have
to generate and maintain

00:22:58.800 --> 00:22:59.730
shifting schema.

00:22:59.730 --> 00:23:01.920
So went with NoSQL.

00:23:01.920 --> 00:23:04.880
And two, we want to be
true microservices, which

00:23:04.880 --> 00:23:08.000
means that every single one
of our little functions,

00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:11.570
every single service has
its own database, right?

00:23:11.570 --> 00:23:13.790
So if you're telling me,
hey, we have currently,

00:23:13.790 --> 00:23:15.920
I think, in our
connected dots, right,

00:23:15.920 --> 00:23:18.980
our Pub/Subs to Cloud Functions
to Pub/Subs to Cloud Functions,

00:23:18.980 --> 00:23:24.020
I think there's currently
89 dots, 89 functions that

00:23:24.020 --> 00:23:26.595
have 89 databases.

00:23:26.595 --> 00:23:27.570
And how do we do that?

00:23:27.570 --> 00:23:29.180
Well, we use Google's Datastore.

00:23:29.180 --> 00:23:31.367
So Datastore is they're
not an SQL thing.

00:23:31.367 --> 00:23:32.700
And actually in Cloud functions.

00:23:32.700 --> 00:23:36.590
I can take any object, just,
literally, a chunk of JSON

00:23:36.590 --> 00:23:37.430
object.

00:23:37.430 --> 00:23:39.755
And I can just say,
save it to Datastore,

00:23:39.755 --> 00:23:40.630
and it's that simple.

00:23:40.630 --> 00:23:42.050
It's literally one line of code.

00:23:42.050 --> 00:23:44.060
And I'm just taking data,
and I'm putting it in.

00:23:44.060 --> 00:23:46.380
And then when I need to get
it later, I pull it out.

00:23:46.380 --> 00:23:50.030
And so you get this synergy,
this connectivity between all

00:23:50.030 --> 00:23:51.944
of these different
services, Pub/Subs

00:23:51.944 --> 00:23:54.110
feeding the Cloud Functions,
Cloud Functions leading

00:23:54.110 --> 00:23:55.720
into Datastore.

00:23:55.720 --> 00:23:57.920
And the best thing
about it is it's

00:23:57.920 --> 00:24:03.344
a incredibly scalable and
super cheap, like crazy cheap.

00:24:03.344 --> 00:24:04.760
And that's the
thing that makes it

00:24:04.760 --> 00:24:08.030
so that you can be fearless
with your databases.

00:24:08.030 --> 00:24:10.400
That's the thing that I
think I really, really like.

00:24:10.400 --> 00:24:11.900
When you're sitting
there-- and I've

00:24:11.900 --> 00:24:14.540
used different
databases for everything

00:24:14.540 --> 00:24:16.706
you can imagine across
every cloud service.

00:24:16.706 --> 00:24:18.080
But the thing that
you want to be

00:24:18.080 --> 00:24:19.230
is you want to be
really fearless.

00:24:19.230 --> 00:24:21.290
You want to be, hey,
I've got to do service.

00:24:21.290 --> 00:24:22.040
I just spun it up.

00:24:22.040 --> 00:24:25.400
It does nothing but
take in the data that's

00:24:25.400 --> 00:24:28.040
coming in from a
sensor, and it checks

00:24:28.040 --> 00:24:30.260
if the IR is within
a known range.

00:24:30.260 --> 00:24:31.550
Otherwise it sends an alert.

00:24:31.550 --> 00:24:33.549
And I just want to maintain
everything it's ever

00:24:33.549 --> 00:24:35.060
read in a database, right?

00:24:35.060 --> 00:24:36.837
In the old days,
you'd be like, ooh,

00:24:36.837 --> 00:24:38.420
how many messages
is that going to be?

00:24:38.420 --> 00:24:42.050
Mm, well, I probably average
around 1,000 a minute, right?

00:24:42.050 --> 00:24:44.512
And you're like, ooh,
that's going to add up.

00:24:44.512 --> 00:24:45.470
Maybe we'll archive it.

00:24:45.470 --> 00:24:46.740
Maybe we're going
to do something.

00:24:46.740 --> 00:24:48.230
But what you can do is
you can say, no, actually,

00:24:48.230 --> 00:24:49.670
just put it in Datastore, right?

00:24:49.670 --> 00:24:50.910
It's super easy.

00:24:50.910 --> 00:24:52.600
It's super cost-effective.

00:24:52.600 --> 00:24:54.890
It scales great, and it's
just there on demand.

00:24:54.890 --> 00:24:56.541
So that the things that we love.

00:24:56.541 --> 00:24:58.040
It's just the one
extra thing that I

00:24:58.040 --> 00:24:58.930
don't have to think about.

00:24:58.930 --> 00:25:00.610
I don't have to think about
maintaining databases.

00:25:00.610 --> 00:25:02.957
I don't think about, oh, how
are our costs going to go?

00:25:02.957 --> 00:25:04.290
And I mentioned the cost, right?

00:25:04.290 --> 00:25:06.740
It's like I've looked
at the numbers for it

00:25:06.740 --> 00:25:09.640
this morning about some of
what our cost things and stuff.

00:25:09.640 --> 00:25:12.170
Yeah, so for example,
our dev site--

00:25:12.170 --> 00:25:13.940
so remember we use Pub/Sub.

00:25:13.940 --> 00:25:15.680
So Pub/Sub's the
queuing service.

00:25:15.680 --> 00:25:19.490
We have a third of a terabyte
of data on the dev-- on the dev,

00:25:19.490 --> 00:25:20.990
not the staging,
not the production,

00:25:20.990 --> 00:25:22.700
on our dev one, where
we're just kind of running

00:25:22.700 --> 00:25:23.783
test data and stuff there.

00:25:23.783 --> 00:25:25.430
So we're running
through about a third

00:25:25.430 --> 00:25:28.460
of a terabyte of data going
through Pub/Sub there.

00:25:28.460 --> 00:25:32.240
And I think it's like, yeah,
$16 to push all that data.

00:25:32.240 --> 00:25:34.600
So you're looking at
that scale, right?

00:25:34.600 --> 00:25:36.740
You're saying, just
I'm getting data.

00:25:36.740 --> 00:25:38.780
I'm branching it like crazy.

00:25:38.780 --> 00:25:40.407
I mean, everywhere,
every event is

00:25:40.407 --> 00:25:42.740
going into a different Pub/Sub
so that you can subscribe

00:25:42.740 --> 00:25:43.823
to these different events.

00:25:43.823 --> 00:25:46.040
It cost us $16 for
a month of the data

00:25:46.040 --> 00:25:47.300
we were pushing through there.

00:25:47.300 --> 00:25:49.799
For the Pub/Subs, so
those little dots,

00:25:49.799 --> 00:25:51.590
the little micro services
that do one thing

00:25:51.590 --> 00:25:53.510
and do them really
well, in September

00:25:53.510 --> 00:25:56.180
we had 96 million invocations.

00:25:56.180 --> 00:25:59.960
That was 3,300 processing hours.

00:25:59.960 --> 00:26:05.480
So we were using 3,300 of
our serverless server time.

00:26:05.480 --> 00:26:06.410
All that together?

00:26:06.410 --> 00:26:07.550
$130.

00:26:07.550 --> 00:26:10.610
So this is literally
the data that we're

00:26:10.610 --> 00:26:13.250
getting from just
deluges of data coming

00:26:13.250 --> 00:26:17.330
in from these sensors telling us
the readings every few seconds,

00:26:17.330 --> 00:26:19.460
every minute, depending
on the device.

00:26:19.460 --> 00:26:22.964
It costs is $130 to manage,
to process that data.

00:26:22.964 --> 00:26:25.380
And then you say, all right,
where are we going to put it?

00:26:25.380 --> 00:26:27.450
We're going to put it
in good, old Datastore.

00:26:27.450 --> 00:26:29.810
We think we had
around 200 million

00:26:29.810 --> 00:26:31.900
reads going through the system.

00:26:31.900 --> 00:26:33.440
So we're storing all that data.

00:26:33.440 --> 00:26:35.660
We're reading it
200 million times.

00:26:35.660 --> 00:26:36.990
We're doing all this stuff.

00:26:36.990 --> 00:26:38.390
And it costs us $100.

00:26:38.390 --> 00:26:41.600
So we're looking at a
sophisticated platform that

00:26:41.600 --> 00:26:46.730
is built to match the
modern architecture,

00:26:46.730 --> 00:26:48.680
this event-driven architecture.

00:26:48.680 --> 00:26:50.870
And you're able to do
it cost-effectively.

00:26:50.870 --> 00:26:52.460
Like the first
time I ever pitched

00:26:52.460 --> 00:26:55.210
event-driven architecture, and
it comes with aggregates, comes

00:26:55.210 --> 00:26:56.960
with all these other
things, I was sitting

00:26:56.960 --> 00:26:59.060
there talking with colleagues.

00:26:59.060 --> 00:27:01.257
And we're going through
it, and we're like, man,

00:27:01.257 --> 00:27:02.840
to do this right,
you're going to have

00:27:02.840 --> 00:27:04.460
to have a ton of databases.

00:27:04.460 --> 00:27:06.890
You're going to have to
have tons of systems.

00:27:06.890 --> 00:27:08.410
You're going to have to
have tons of these things.

00:27:08.410 --> 00:27:09.860
You're like, the cost is
just going to be too much.

00:27:09.860 --> 00:27:11.930
It's just like, it's
a great architecture.

00:27:11.930 --> 00:27:13.182
It makes a lot of sense.

00:27:13.182 --> 00:27:15.140
But it doesn't make a
lot of sense financially.

00:27:15.140 --> 00:27:17.304
And that was one of the
really painful moments

00:27:17.304 --> 00:27:18.470
being a professional, right?

00:27:18.470 --> 00:27:19.700
You're like, I got it.

00:27:19.700 --> 00:27:20.600
I got the secret.

00:27:20.600 --> 00:27:22.520
I can do this thing using
event-driven architecture.

00:27:22.520 --> 00:27:23.840
But then when you look
at it, you're like,

00:27:23.840 --> 00:27:24.950
it's too expensive.

00:27:24.950 --> 00:27:26.480
Just go do it the old way.

00:27:26.480 --> 00:27:31.460
But with GCP and Pub/Sub and
Cloud Functions and Datastore,

00:27:31.460 --> 00:27:33.920
you're actually free
to make these kinds

00:27:33.920 --> 00:27:37.310
of new-era architectures, these
event-driven architectures, all

00:27:37.310 --> 00:27:38.810
these other ones
that are out there.

00:27:38.810 --> 00:27:40.880
Really say, hey,
if the domain, it's

00:27:40.880 --> 00:27:43.910
the main that's going to decide
what I'm to do, not the costs.

00:27:43.910 --> 00:27:45.860
And that's really
liberating, right?

00:27:45.860 --> 00:27:49.840
So I am literally taking
just deluges of data,

00:27:49.840 --> 00:27:50.840
and I'm just storing it.

00:27:50.840 --> 00:27:52.780
And I'm not just
storing it once.

00:27:52.780 --> 00:27:55.040
I may store it
multiple times, right?

00:27:55.040 --> 00:27:57.110
I might say, well, I
got it in, and here's

00:27:57.110 --> 00:27:58.380
information about the device.

00:27:58.380 --> 00:28:01.842
It branched to another command,
another Cloud Function.

00:28:01.842 --> 00:28:04.300
And it's seeing the same thing,
and they're both saving it,

00:28:04.300 --> 00:28:04.799
right?

00:28:04.799 --> 00:28:06.864
And if you told me
literally a year ago,

00:28:06.864 --> 00:28:08.780
hey, Brian, you're going
to duplicate, triple,

00:28:08.780 --> 00:28:10.460
replicate all this
data, and you're just

00:28:10.460 --> 00:28:11.876
going to put it
all over the place

00:28:11.876 --> 00:28:13.560
and you're not going
to be afraid of it,

00:28:13.560 --> 00:28:15.476
I'd be like, yeah, that's
a pipe dream, right?

00:28:15.476 --> 00:28:18.370
No, I'm going to have my
one row per device, right?

00:28:18.370 --> 00:28:20.195
I'm not going maintain
a time series,

00:28:20.195 --> 00:28:23.990
does literally every single
change in the record,

00:28:23.990 --> 00:28:26.330
and I'm going to do it
across multiple systems,

00:28:26.330 --> 00:28:29.360
and I'll be able to do it,
write it, pull it, access it

00:28:29.360 --> 00:28:31.060
for $130?

00:28:31.060 --> 00:28:31.890
Never.

00:28:31.890 --> 00:28:33.620
I just would have thought it
never would have happened.

00:28:33.620 --> 00:28:34.850
But Google gives
you that chance.

00:28:34.850 --> 00:28:35.140
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:28:35.140 --> 00:28:35.460
All right.

00:28:35.460 --> 00:28:36.990
We are running out
of time, so I just

00:28:36.990 --> 00:28:38.642
