00:28:52.130 --> 00:28:52.630
[LAUGHS]

00:28:52.630 --> 00:28:52.980
BRIAN GRANATIR: Yeah.

00:28:52.980 --> 00:28:53.330
Yeah.

00:28:53.330 --> 00:28:55.270
MARK MANDEL: How do you
make that stuff work?

00:28:55.270 --> 00:28:56.010
BRIAN GRANATIR: And
that's the other thing.

00:28:56.010 --> 00:28:58.220
So I'm glad you brought that up,
because one of the things that

00:28:58.220 --> 00:28:59.130
is you get the data in.

00:28:59.130 --> 00:28:59.900
You store the data.

00:28:59.900 --> 00:29:01.030
But then how do you act upon it?

00:29:01.030 --> 00:29:02.360
How do you get that meaning?

00:29:02.360 --> 00:29:05.270
How do you say, oh,
well, I want to take data

00:29:05.270 --> 00:29:07.190
on the devices that
were telling me parking,

00:29:07.190 --> 00:29:09.260
and I want to co-locate it
with the devices from traffic,

00:29:09.260 --> 00:29:09.430
right?

00:29:09.430 --> 00:29:10.320
How do I do that?

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:12.230
And this is where
BigQuery comes in.

00:29:12.230 --> 00:29:14.090
So BigQuery is
probably the granddaddy

00:29:14.090 --> 00:29:15.200
of all Google services.

00:29:15.200 --> 00:29:17.210
This is probably the
reason most people

00:29:17.210 --> 00:29:18.330
listen to these podcasts.

00:29:18.330 --> 00:29:20.371
This is probably the reason
most people initially

00:29:20.371 --> 00:29:21.590
go look at GCP.

00:29:21.590 --> 00:29:26.870
BigQuery is their true
big data analytics system.

00:29:26.870 --> 00:29:30.710
And it basically allows you to
go over-- and I've seen demos,

00:29:30.710 --> 00:29:33.050
and I've done it myself, where
you can go over terabytes

00:29:33.050 --> 00:29:34.400
and terabytes of data.

00:29:34.400 --> 00:29:36.140
You can run these
massive queries,

00:29:36.140 --> 00:29:38.750
and they'll respond
in five seconds,

00:29:38.750 --> 00:29:41.052
just going over these
just huge data sets.

00:29:41.052 --> 00:29:42.260
And so that's the key, right?

00:29:42.260 --> 00:29:43.730
So we get it Datastore.

00:29:43.730 --> 00:29:46.490
But we also replicate
it into BigQuery.

00:29:46.490 --> 00:29:50.150
So I have a non-SQL database,
which is really easy for me

00:29:50.150 --> 00:29:51.140
to interact with.

00:29:51.140 --> 00:29:52.700
It's super easy to do it.

00:29:52.700 --> 00:29:54.290
And then I find
aggregates, which

00:29:54.290 --> 00:29:56.090
is another event-driven
architecture

00:29:56.090 --> 00:29:58.790
system, terminology
rather, that says

00:29:58.790 --> 00:30:00.440
I'm co-locating
certain events to make

00:30:00.440 --> 00:30:02.210
an aggregate, a
view of something.

00:30:02.210 --> 00:30:04.340
So I take those aggregate,
and put them into it

00:30:04.340 --> 00:30:07.160
SQL database, start
putting them in BigQuery.

00:30:07.160 --> 00:30:11.630
And from there, I can go and do
these huge, wonderful queries

00:30:11.630 --> 00:30:13.550
across these massive data sets.

00:30:13.550 --> 00:30:14.900
And that's another thing, too.

00:30:14.900 --> 00:30:16.700
Go back a year and
tell me, hey, Brian,

00:30:16.700 --> 00:30:17.430
you're going to take data.

00:30:17.430 --> 00:30:19.054
You're not only going
to put it in just

00:30:19.054 --> 00:30:21.050
tons of non-SQL
databases, but you're also

00:30:21.050 --> 00:30:24.230
going to replicate it
in an SQL database just

00:30:24.230 --> 00:30:27.362
to make it easy to query because
people know how to write a SQL.

00:30:27.362 --> 00:30:28.820
They know how to
do all that stuff.

00:30:28.820 --> 00:30:30.350
So being able to
say, hey, you're

00:30:30.350 --> 00:30:33.019
going to do those two things
because it's cost-efficient, I

00:30:33.019 --> 00:30:35.060
just would have said like,
ah, that's the future.

00:30:35.060 --> 00:30:37.000
There's no way that's
going to happen.

00:30:37.000 --> 00:30:38.540
But it's right here.

00:30:38.540 --> 00:30:40.662
We've been doing it, and
it's just been amazing.

00:30:40.662 --> 00:30:42.120
And there's another
thing that goes

00:30:42.120 --> 00:30:43.220
hand-in-hand with it, right?

00:30:43.220 --> 00:30:44.120
You get the data in there.

00:30:44.120 --> 00:30:46.190
You query it, but how do
you make it look good?

00:30:46.190 --> 00:30:47.872
So Google has Data Studio.

00:30:47.872 --> 00:30:49.580
Data Studio allows
you to say, hey, well,

00:30:49.580 --> 00:30:50.931
I've got data in BigQuery.

00:30:50.931 --> 00:30:52.430
I want to make it
look like a graph.

00:30:52.430 --> 00:30:53.930
I want to make it
look like a chart.

00:30:53.930 --> 00:30:55.490
I want to make a dashboard.

00:30:55.490 --> 00:30:58.010
And it's really easy for
people to go through and do it.

00:30:58.010 --> 00:30:59.810
I tell people we had a
project manager on our team,

00:30:59.810 --> 00:31:01.400
so nothing against
project managers.

00:31:01.400 --> 00:31:02.450
Love me project managers.

00:31:02.450 --> 00:31:04.130
But we had a project manager.

00:31:04.130 --> 00:31:05.630
She sat down.

00:31:05.630 --> 00:31:09.350
And we said, here's Data Studio
tucked into our occupancy

00:31:09.350 --> 00:31:10.250
parking data.

00:31:10.250 --> 00:31:10.850
Go nuts.

00:31:10.850 --> 00:31:13.360
And she sat down and
learned Data Studio, learned

00:31:13.360 --> 00:31:15.800
how to query it through
our massive data sets,

00:31:15.800 --> 00:31:19.400
and she made a whole report,
and it took her a day, right?

00:31:19.400 --> 00:31:21.860
And she's not like
an expert in SQL.

00:31:21.860 --> 00:31:23.690
She's not an expert in
any of these things.

00:31:23.690 --> 00:31:26.752
She just sat down, connected
the dots for the data stores,

00:31:26.752 --> 00:31:28.460
made some graphs, and
was able to present

00:31:28.460 --> 00:31:30.510
a report to one of our clients.

00:31:30.510 --> 00:31:31.580
And you're like, wow.

00:31:31.580 --> 00:31:35.620
So this is often referred to
as the democratization of data.

00:31:35.620 --> 00:31:38.780
This is data that's
not only available,

00:31:38.780 --> 00:31:41.240
but it's not going to
cost you an arm and a leg

00:31:41.240 --> 00:31:42.530
to do a query over it.

00:31:42.530 --> 00:31:45.590
And it responds in a way that
anyone could access it, not

00:31:45.590 --> 00:31:48.680
just us super geeks, right?

00:31:48.680 --> 00:31:52.130
Project managers, data
analysts, marketing PR people,

00:31:52.130 --> 00:31:53.150
they can all do it.

